.TH MWR 1 "" "mwr" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mwr \- Mergewright, a terminal Git client for the daily work
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mwr
.RB [ "\-h, \-\-help" ]
.RB [ "\-V, \-\-version" ]
.RB [ "\-\-generate\-man" ]
.RI [ path ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
mwr opens the Git repository at path, or the one the current directory is in,
and draws its history as a graph. Staging, committing, branching, merging,
rebasing, stashing, fetching, pulling and pushing are done from the keyboard.
What it changes in the repository is written to an undo journal, and u undoes
the last change.
.PP
A bar at the bottom of the screen names the keys that work where you are. Press
? for help on the place you are in, and ctrl+p for the command palette, which
finds every action by name.
.PP
With no repository, mwr shows where you can go instead: a recent repository, a
clone, or a practice repository with a short tour.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I path
The repository to open. Default: the current directory. After --, every argument
is a path, even one that starts with a dash.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print the usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-generate\-man
Print the man page (roff) to stdout and exit.
.SH KEYS
Every action, by the place it works in. Keys are written the way the [keys]
tables of the config file take them; the name after each label is the action's
name there. An action listed with no key has none until you give it one.
